What was rewritten: the routing engine of public/hermes-jev-routing (jev.py,
decide.py, config.py, ledger.py, pricing.py, skip.py, notice.py, turn.py),
rewritten in Common Lisp. The four typed questions (task_kind, complexity,
capability_deserved, needs_deep_reasoning) and their instructions, the state
they read, the demand score, the kind floor, the confidence fallback, the
xpremium tier, the budget policy, the chain order (kind chain, the tier, its
neighbours, the free pool) and the first row that names a model are the
original's, line for line where Lisp allows. The spend ledger beside the
routing table keeps the original's shape, its rate card and its token
accounting. The one line the original prints under the composer became the
verb's :line.

What was left out, and why: the turn itself. A Hermes plugin reads the
prompt before the model starts and rewrites the provider request, or moves
the session through the app's own model switch; Nodecode hands a cell no
per-turn model hook, so this cell is the decision engine the turn would have
used, called through eval, and it advises instead of rewriting. Also left
out: the Hermes host's provider catalogue, quota windows and cache hold, the
approve strip and its confirm clicks, session pinning and stickiness, the
desktop half (desktop/plugin.js), `/jev-routing suggest` and the ranking and
generated-layer half of ranking.py, and the Postgres/pricing reads the
personal edition makes. A cell reads no catalogue, so every configured row
is eligible, exactly as the plugin's own catalog edition says of itself.
